  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Five different civilisations have been credited with carving one boulder. Dighton Rock. Forty tons of sandstone, hauled out of a river in Massachusetts.",
        "visual_query": "massachusetts river boulder new england",
        "visual_fallback": "river boulder"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A minister sketched its marks in 1680, and that drawing sits in the British Museum. Cotton Mather wrote the rock up ten years later.",
        "visual_query": "colonial new england engraving archive",
        "visual_fallback": "old engraving"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then the readings began. Phoenician letters, said Ezra Stiles in 1783. Norsemen, said a Danish scholar in 1837. Chinese sailors, said an author in 2002.",
        "visual_query": "antique nautical charts parchment maps",
        "visual_fallback": "old maps"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A Brown University professor found a Portuguese name in the scratches: Miguel Corte-Real, 1511. A 2017 book pulled that apart line by line.",
        "visual_query": "weathered carved stone petroglyph surface",
        "visual_fallback": "carved stone"
      },
      {
        "narration": "No translation has survived scrutiny. The marks look like the Indigenous petroglyphs found across New England, and the mineral crust grown into the carvings means nobody can date them.",
        "visual_query": "new england forest river rocks",
        "visual_fallback": "forest river"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
    "word_count": 124,
    "duration_seconds": 35.0,
